<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>String of Pearls - Lance the Boil (Sri Lanka), 2020</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=4 face=Arial&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;FONT size=4 face=Arial&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;U&gt;Overview&lt;/U&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=4 face=Arial&gt;The String of Pearls is a geopolitical theory related to Chinese intentions in the Indian Ocean and Africa. It refers to the network of potential Chinese military and commercial facilities of trade which extend from the Chinese mainland to the Atlantic. The lines of &lt;SPAN style="FONT-SIZE: 16pt; FONT-FAMILY: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-bidi-font-size: 11.0pt; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-fareast-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-language: AR-SA"&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;communication &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;run through several major maritime choke points such as the Strait of Mandeb, the Strait of Malacca, the Strait of Hormuz, and the Lombok Strait as well as other strategic maritime centers in Pakistan, Sri Lanka, Bangladesh, the Maldives, and Somalia. The term as was first used in an internal US Department of Defense report, "Energy Futures in Asia." The term has never been used by Chinese government sources, but it is often used in Indian media.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;This is a scenario based off of a Command map,"String of Pearls Project"&amp;nbsp;which &amp;nbsp;includes &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;SPAN style="FONT-SIZE: 16pt; FONT-FAMILY: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-bidi-font-size: 11.0pt; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi"&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;naval bases: Chongjin Port (North Korea), Moresby Port (Papua New Guinea), Sihanoukville Port (Cambodia), Koh Lanta Port (Thailand) Sittwe Port (Myanmar), Dhaka Port (Bangladesh), Gwadar Port (Pakistan), Hambantota Port (Sri Lanka), Maldives, Seychelles, Lagos Port (Nigeria), Mombasa Port (Kenya), Dar es Salaam Port (Tanzania) Djibouti Port (Djibouti Luanda Port (Angola).&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&lt;SPAN style="FONT-SIZE: 16pt; FONT-FAMILY: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-bidi-font-size: 11.0pt; mso-bidi-font-family: 'Times New Roman'; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi"&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = "o" ns = "urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;The present scenario uses Hambantota Port (Sri Lanka). Please see the side briefing for details.&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;Kevin Kinscherf &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;A href="mailto:kevinkin@comcast.net"&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;kevinkin@comcast.net&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/A&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=4 face=Arial&gt;Deni Maggio&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&lt;SPAN style="FONT-SIZE: 12px; FONT-FAMILY: Verdana; WHITE-SPACE: normal; TEXT-TRANSFORM: none; WORD-SPACING: 0px; FLOAT: none; FONT-WEIGHT: normal; COLOR: rgb(0,0,0); FONT-STYLE: normal; ORPHANS: 2; WIDOWS: 2; DISPLAY: inline !important; LETTER-SPACING: normal; BACKGROUND-COLOR: rgb(225,225,225); TEXT-INDENT: 0px; font-variant-ligatures: normal; font-variant-caps: normal; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px"&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
